response:
Sure, a “loop” is an operation that is performed a certain number of times.  There are many different types of loops, but they all work by repeatedly executing a block of code (the “loop body”) while a condition is true.  Here are three examples:

for (a = 0; a < 10; a++) {
    doSomething();
}

This loop executes the code inside the body 10 times, starting with a value of 0 and ending with 9.  It also sets the value of "a" to 10 after it has finished.

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    // do something
}

This is the same as the previous loop, except that "i" is an integer variable and increments by 1 before it has finished.

for (;;) {
    // loop body
}

This loop continues to execute the body as long as "i" is less than 10.  It does not exit until "i" is greater than 10.

